{
  "query": "Riverton Outfitters is planning a new customer portal on Google Cloud. The engineering group must decide whether to use a managed database service or to run and administer the database themselves on virtual machines. Which statement best distinguishes managed database services from self managed databases on Google Cloud?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Managed database services only work with relational databases, while self managed deployments can also run nonrelational engines",
      "explanation": "This statement suggests a feature limitation for hosted databases and broader engine support for customer managed setups.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Managed database services include encryption by default, while self managed databases cannot use encryption for data at rest or in transit",
      "explanation": "This statement implies that security features are exclusive to hosted offerings and unavailable for customer managed databases.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Managed database services handle automatic scaling and routine patching, while self managed databases on Compute Engine require you to plan capacity and scale manually",
      "explanation": "This statement contrasts service operated automation with the manual work typically needed when you run databases on virtual machines.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Managed database services always use a flat monthly fee, while self managed databases are billed only when queries execute",
      "explanation": "This statement describes a pricing model difference between hosted offerings and customer managed deployments.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>Only <b>Managed database services handle automatic scaling and routine patching, while self managed databases on Compute Engine require you to plan capacity and scale manually</b> is correct.</p><p>This choice reflects the managed model where Google operates the service and provides automated maintenance and scaling characteristics, which reduces operational burden. If you run the database on Compute Engine then you must size instances, configure replication and backups, apply patches, and adjust capacity as demand changes.</p><p><i>Managed database services only work with relational databases, while self managed deployments can also run nonrelational engines</i> is wrong because Google provides fully managed nonrelational databases as well. Firestore and Bigtable are examples and they scale automatically without you provisioning servers.</p><p><i>Managed database services include encryption by default, while self managed databases cannot use encryption for data at rest or in transit</i> is wrong because encryption at rest is enabled by default for Google Cloud storage and you can enable TLS for database traffic. You can also use customer managed encryption keys with both approaches to meet compliance needs.</p><p><i>Managed database services always use a flat monthly fee, while self managed databases are billed only when queries execute</i> is wrong because pricing varies by product and is typically based on provisioned or consumed resources. Self managed databases incur costs for the virtual machines, storage, and networking while managed services charge for CPU, memory, storage, and operations rather than per query.</p>",
